  "scope": "Prepared Seq transfer combines the original host-owned accepted prefix with pending identity/domain and unpublished requests through SequenceReplyCore. Exact prefix count, row indices and original IR pointers precede moves; refusal returns queue and prefix intact. There is no native equality, callback, clone, projection, dispatch or fuel change; a bounded row vector allocates. Completed frontiers restore Completed, never runnable. Cancelled/failed/uncertain queues refuse. Shared IR is not execution provenance: native restore admission rejects foreign-execution prefixes. Two hosts transfer original queues before publication, after publication/dispatch and after acceptance through the original VM, preserving exact outputs, remaining fuel, correlation counts and invocations at every budget cutoff. Mixed-helper and bounded byte-checkpoint models use actual original replies and requests instead of rebuilding accepted handles or sample payloads. Seven three-member frontiers, maximum-width boundaries, unsized domains, repeated transfers, native refusals/unwind, malformed private states and cleanup failures are covered. Scope, meter, external cancellation, authentic storage and settlement remain host-owned; no production codec or complete durable graph recovery is added.",
